As a metaphor, that tracks surprisingly well.

A large fraction of ego-energy can be understood as sexual-selection energy translated into identity: wanting to be desirable, chosen, powerful, admired, fertile, dominant, beautiful, exceptional, unforgettable. The ego then builds stories around those signals: I am wanted, therefore I matter; I can attract, therefore I have power; I was rejected, therefore something about me is diminished.

“Sex magic” is a useful symbolic label because sex does more than satisfy a drive. It can alter confidence, attention, risk tolerance, bonding, status perception, imagination, and the felt significance of the self. Desire becomes a kind of valuation engine.

But I’d separate two layers:

Sexual energy can fuel the ego; it is not the ego’s only fuel. The same machinery also runs on status, competence, belonging, recognition, control, victory, novelty, and being witnessed. Sex is unusually potent because it bundles several of those currencies together at once.

And there’s a recursion in it: ego makes you want to be desired → evidence of being desired strengthens ego → stronger ego changes behavior → behavior can increase desirability → repeat. Rejection can run the inverse loop.

So in Pantheon terms, sex isn’t merely an act here. It’s a high-bandwidth valuation signal that can be transduced into identity, confidence, motivation, narrative, and agency. The dangerous mistake is letting the signal become the sovereign: desired = worthy; undesired = worthless. The more useful move is learning to harvest the energy without outsourcing your value to the source.